          The following libraries are provided:

          Library Name                Description

          libbti1553.so               MIL-STD-1553 driver
          libbti422.so                ARINC 422 driver
          libbti429.so                ARINC 429 driver
          libbti708.so                ARINC 708 driver
          libbti717.so                ARINC 717 driver
          libbtiCSDB.so               CSDB driver
          libbtiDIO.so                DIO driver
          libbtiEBR.so                EBR driver

          libbtiCard.so               CARD driver
          libbtiCard_debug.so         Debug build of CARD driver

          ballard.conf                Example ballard.conf

          Refer to the end of this document for corrections,
          warnings, and additional information.



     INTRODUCTION

          BTIDriver is a unified Application Programming
          Interface (API) for Ballard Avionics
          Databus Interface PCI, USB, and Ethernet devices.
          These avionics databus interfaces include
          MIL-STD-1553, ARINC 429, ARINC 717, ARINC 708,
          and others.  The folders listed above contain
          the necessary components for building applications
          for BTIDriver compliant devices.



     USAGE

          The example programs included with this driver can
          be used as a starting point for developing your own
          application.  They demonstrate various capabilities
          and programming methods of the BTIDriver API.

          Before you can run the example programs, you will
          first need to install the API libraries.  Copy the
          following shared object libraries to the /usr/lib
          directory of your computer (as root):

          libbtiCard.so
          libbti1553.so
          libbti422.so
          libbti429.so
          libbti708.so
          libbti717.so
          libbtiCSDB.so
          libbtiDIO.so
          libbtiEBR.so



     PCI KERNEL DRIVER

         To operate BTIDriver compatible PCI cards, the
         PCI kernel driver must be installed.  Refer to the
         <BTIKPCI> directory for building and installing
         the PCI kernel driver.

         The PCI kernel driver is not needed if a PCI card
         is not being operated.



     USB KERNEL DRIVER

         To operate BTIDriver compatible USB cards, the
         USB kernel driver must be installed.  Refer to the
         <BTIKUSB> directory for building and installing
         the USB kernel driver.

         The USB kernel driver is not needed if a USB card
         is not being operated.

         As an alternative, BTIDriver compatible USB cards
         can be accessed using the libusb-1.0 library.  Refer to
         the <BTIDRIVER_LIBUSB1> directory for the BTIDriver
         files compatible with the libusb-1.0 library.



     BALLARD.CONF CARD NUMBER ASSIGNMENTS

          The file /etc/ballard.conf assigns card numbers
          to BTIDriver devices.  If not present,
          cards are assumed to be numbered starting at
          zero, first the PCI cards, and then the USB
          cards.  ballard.conf must be present to access
          Ethernet devices.


          PCI CARDS

               The starting card number for all PCI cards
               can be set.  If none is specified, then all
               PCI cards are in system order starting at zero.

               If an Ethernet device is assigned the same card
               number as a PCI device, the Ethernet device takes
               precedence.

              The format should be:

                   <CARDNUM>,pcistart


          USB CARDS

               The starting card number for all USB cards can
               be set.  If none is specified, then all USB
               cards are in system order starting after the
               last PCI card, or zero if there are no PCI cards.

               If an Ethernet device is assigned the same card number
               as a USB device, the RPC device takes precedence.
               Likewise, if a PCI card is assigned the same card number
               as a USB device, the PCI device takes precedence.

              The format should be:

                   <CARDNUM>,usbstart


          RPC DEVICES

               Lines in ballard.conf map a card number to a particular
               BTIDriver Ethernet device.  Either a MAC address, an IPv4
               address, or an IPv6 address can be specified.  Use an IP
               address only if a static IP address has been assigned to
               the device.

               The format should be:

                   <CARDNUM>,<MAC_ADDRESS>
                   <CARDNUM>,<IPV4_ADDRESS>
                   <CARDNUM>,<IPV6_ADDRESS>

               Note: Not all BTIDriver Ethernet devices support IPv6.
               Contact Customer Support for additional information.



     MOVING AND RENAMING BALLARD.CONF

          If the environment variable BTI_CONFIG_FILE is defined,
          its value is used as the path and filename to use
          instead of the file /etc/ballard.conf.



     FIREWALL CONFIGURATION

          The driver can broadcast a request on the network to find
          RPC devices that are present.  Responses to this request
          can be blocked by the firewall.  The firewall must be
          configured to allow these responses on port 111 to be
          accepted.  The following command will open port 111:

               iptables -I INPUT 1 -p UDP --sport 111 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j ACCEPT

          Refer to the btirpc_find example.



     CORRECTIONS, WARNINGS, AND ADDITIONAL INFORMATION

          There is no additional information.


